WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:02.370
Instructor: So now we finally get to talk about Commits.

00:02.370 --> 00:04.080
Our good friend, we've been working

00:04.080 --> 00:05.790
with Commits for quite a long time.

00:05.790 --> 00:07.740
We've seen these commit hashes

00:07.740 --> 00:10.380
but now let's talk about the actual underlying structure

00:10.380 --> 00:12.870
of the Git object, the commit.

00:12.870 --> 00:16.287
So when we make a commits, Git generates a new commit,

00:16.287 --> 00:19.050
Git object, and it stores that, just like it stores

00:19.050 --> 00:21.480
all the other objects the blobs and trees we've seen,

00:21.480 --> 00:24.450
it hashes them it makes a file over here

00:24.450 --> 00:26.070
in the objects directory.

00:26.070 --> 00:27.810
First two digits make a folder.

00:27.810 --> 00:31.050
Inside of that 38 digits of the hash that are remaining

00:31.050 --> 00:34.380
are the name of that encrypted binary compressed file.

00:34.380 --> 00:36.720
Okay, so commits are going to be stored in there as well.

00:36.720 --> 00:37.950
What we haven't really talked about

00:37.950 --> 00:40.110
is what a commit actually contains.

00:40.110 --> 00:41.850
We know that it contains a message

00:41.850 --> 00:43.590
because we provide that message.

00:43.590 --> 00:45.750
We've seen that it includes the author

00:45.750 --> 00:48.030
but what we have not really talked about

00:48.030 --> 00:50.490
is the fact that commits also include a reference

00:50.490 --> 00:52.170
to a parent commit.

00:52.170 --> 00:53.760
We've mentioned that,

00:53.760 --> 00:55.470
but we haven't gone into a ton of detail.

00:55.470 --> 00:57.480
So that's a reference to the hash

00:57.480 --> 00:59.340
of the commit that came before.

00:59.340 --> 01:01.650
So really all we need to get the entire chain,

01:01.650 --> 01:05.370
the entire history of a Git repo is just a commits

01:05.370 --> 01:06.900
that links back to a parent

01:06.900 --> 01:08.460
and then we can go look at that parent,

01:08.460 --> 01:09.900
it links back to another parent

01:09.900 --> 01:11.760
and that links back to another parent.

01:11.760 --> 01:14.220
Anyway, commits have a reference to the parent commits

01:14.220 --> 01:19.220
but also they have a reference to a tree object.

01:19.290 --> 01:21.510
So this tree object is just like

01:21.510 --> 01:24.630
the tree objects that we've seen, like this,

01:24.630 --> 01:28.710
where a tree can reference other trees and also blobs.

01:28.710 --> 01:31.050
So you could visualize it this way instead,

01:31.050 --> 01:32.610
every commit has a hash, right?

01:32.610 --> 01:36.090
All the contents are hashed to generate this.

01:36.090 --> 01:38.430
There is the parent at the beginning of this repo.

01:38.430 --> 01:39.450
There's no history.

01:39.450 --> 01:42.450
So a parent just doesn't exist on this one commits.

01:42.450 --> 01:45.510
There's an author committer, there's a message.

01:45.510 --> 01:47.310
But the tree is the most important part

01:47.310 --> 01:48.630
that I want to talk about.

01:48.630 --> 01:50.730
When we run Git Commit,

01:50.730 --> 01:53.190
Git creates this new commit object for us

01:53.190 --> 01:55.980
and it sets the parent to be the current head commit.

01:55.980 --> 01:58.350
So that makes sense probably, right?

01:58.350 --> 02:00.150
If we make a new commit,

02:00.150 --> 02:02.880
it's going to have the parent of our current head.

02:02.880 --> 02:05.493
So it would go over here and point back to this one.

02:06.420 --> 02:10.590
But also, the tree that it stores in this commit,

02:10.590 --> 02:13.170
the tree is the current content of the index,

02:13.170 --> 02:14.490
the staging area.

02:14.490 --> 02:17.610
So there's an actual index file, but basically,

02:17.610 --> 02:20.370
everything that we have worked on, all our changes,

02:20.370 --> 02:23.580
everything we have staged and prepared to commit,

02:23.580 --> 02:27.660
when we actually run, Git Commits a tree is generated.

02:27.660 --> 02:31.740
So that tree will reflect the contents of the index

02:31.740 --> 02:34.170
and then that is included in the commit.

02:34.170 --> 02:37.260
Then we have the parent that references the parent commits.

02:37.260 --> 02:39.570
So that's giving us the history, right?

02:39.570 --> 02:42.840
So this is giving us the actual contents of the directory,

02:42.840 --> 02:45.930
our changes, and that tree is going to include,

02:45.930 --> 02:48.093
most likely other trees and blobs.

02:49.050 --> 02:52.110
And then there's the metadata, author, committer, message.

02:52.110 --> 02:53.520
And then that whole thing is hashed.

02:53.520 --> 02:56.040
And we end up with this commit hash.

02:56.040 --> 02:58.890
So if I make a new commit, remember what I said here,

02:58.890 --> 03:00.900
that when we make a new commit,

03:00.900 --> 03:03.960
the head commit is going to be the parent

03:03.960 --> 03:04.800
of that new commit.

03:04.800 --> 03:06.630
So this was head right here,

03:06.630 --> 03:09.180
now head is here and it points back,

03:09.180 --> 03:11.790
this commit refers back to the commit before it,

03:11.790 --> 03:12.623
the parent.

03:12.623 --> 03:15.120
So every commit is tied to a tree

03:15.120 --> 03:17.700
and that tree is something that represents

03:17.700 --> 03:19.680
the structure of the application.

03:19.680 --> 03:22.590
And in turn, the blobs that it contains all the data

03:22.590 --> 03:23.850
in the files.

03:23.850 --> 03:26.040
So we could go look at any commit

03:26.040 --> 03:29.070
and we can see that tree for that commit.

03:29.070 --> 03:30.870
We can take a look, we can inspect it.

03:30.870 --> 03:33.630
And then of course if we check out a commit,

03:33.630 --> 03:35.760
if we make a new branch based upon a commit,

03:35.760 --> 03:38.970
Git is going to take that tree and use it as the basis

03:38.970 --> 03:41.100
for our working directory.

03:41.100 --> 03:42.810
So let me demonstrate that.

03:42.810 --> 03:46.080
Lemme just show you an example here, another diagram.

03:46.080 --> 03:47.160
So here's this structure,

03:47.160 --> 03:50.730
we've talked about trees referring to blobs and to trees

03:50.730 --> 03:54.480
but a commit includes one tree.

03:54.480 --> 03:56.400
So this would be the state of our application

03:56.400 --> 03:58.440
at this time of the commit.

03:58.440 --> 04:00.510
And then we have the commit message and so on.

04:00.510 --> 04:03.000
So every commit has a tree like that.

04:03.000 --> 04:05.550
So here's a much more expanded diagram

04:05.550 --> 04:07.770
that just shows that the details, right?

04:07.770 --> 04:09.780
If you look at the hashes all match.

04:09.780 --> 04:14.430
So this commit, its tree, it's snapshot C387,

04:14.430 --> 04:16.080
that's this one right here.

04:16.080 --> 04:19.350
And then the tree has blobs and trees and so on.

04:19.350 --> 04:22.230
So let me try to demonstrate this.

04:22.230 --> 04:23.850
Let's make a simple repo.

04:23.850 --> 04:24.870
We'll just start from...

04:24.870 --> 04:27.360
Well we've got this dog repo already.

04:27.360 --> 04:29.010
Right? This one I have open.

04:29.010 --> 04:30.723
I haven't made any commits.

04:31.800 --> 04:33.570
So if you wanna follow along, right?

04:33.570 --> 04:35.940
If you have a new repo, you haven't made any commits,

04:35.940 --> 04:37.020
that will work for us.

04:37.020 --> 04:39.420
So right now there are no commit objects.

04:39.420 --> 04:43.560
I have added manually, I've added some blobs

04:43.560 --> 04:46.080
but you don't need those for this demonstration.

04:46.080 --> 04:49.380
What we do need to do is make an initial commit.

04:49.380 --> 04:53.550
So I have this one file dogs.txt.

04:53.550 --> 04:55.440
Why don't I just commit that?

04:55.440 --> 04:59.100
So I'll do something like Git add dogs.txt,

04:59.100 --> 05:03.393
Git commit, add dogs file or initial commit or something.

05:04.380 --> 05:05.490
Okay?

05:05.490 --> 05:07.440
So I just made a commit.

05:07.440 --> 05:10.590
And if we look at that commit with Git log,

05:10.590 --> 05:13.290
we see the commit hash, we see the author,

05:13.290 --> 05:15.120
we see the date and the message.

05:15.120 --> 05:16.863
But there is more to this commit.

05:17.700 --> 05:21.810
If I copy this hash, we can use the same command we've seen,

05:21.810 --> 05:24.540
cat file, Git cat file

05:24.540 --> 05:27.420
and then let's do dash T for type.

05:27.420 --> 05:30.660
And we see that we have a commit object.

05:30.660 --> 05:34.470
Also, 79DD6, let's remember that.

05:34.470 --> 05:36.960
Over here, what do we have?

05:36.960 --> 05:39.000
79, whoops, there it is.

05:39.000 --> 05:42.540
In the objects directory of dot Git, DD6.

05:42.540 --> 05:45.090
This is where my commit is stored.

05:45.090 --> 05:47.490
But I can actually view much more about it.

05:47.490 --> 05:50.610
I can pretty print it using Git cat file dash P,

05:50.610 --> 05:51.720
which we've also seen.

05:51.720 --> 05:54.720
We've printed trees, we printed objects or rather blobs,

05:54.720 --> 05:55.980
they're all objects.

05:55.980 --> 05:57.033
This is a commit.

05:57.990 --> 06:00.870
And now what we see is a little bit different.

06:00.870 --> 06:03.030
When we pretty print this commit hash

06:03.030 --> 06:06.300
we actually end up seeing the tree.

06:06.300 --> 06:10.200
So remember, every commit contains a reference to a tree

06:10.200 --> 06:13.680
and this tree represents the structure of our application,

06:13.680 --> 06:17.280
the snapshot of our directory, the app,

06:17.280 --> 06:20.070
at this point in time when we made the commit.

06:20.070 --> 06:22.110
So we could copy this

06:22.110 --> 06:25.230
and you'll see different hashes almost guaranteed,

06:25.230 --> 06:26.640
but if you are following along,

06:26.640 --> 06:28.740
we can verify that is indeed a tree, right?

06:28.740 --> 06:33.270
If we do Git cat file dash T for type, it's a tree.

06:33.270 --> 06:36.213
But I can also do Git cat file dash p.

06:37.410 --> 06:39.630
And we see, okay, here's the output

06:39.630 --> 06:41.880
or here's the contents, rather, of the tree

06:41.880 --> 06:44.880
just one file dogs.txt.

06:44.880 --> 06:47.700
But now, let's make a second commit

06:47.700 --> 06:49.350
and let's do some other changes.

06:49.350 --> 06:51.393
Why don't we make another file in here?

06:52.290 --> 06:54.930
So what do we have, dogs?

06:54.930 --> 06:56.700
Let's just do, you know, cats?

06:56.700 --> 06:57.960
I'll just do it from the command line.

06:57.960 --> 07:02.960
So I'm gonna do echo, meow, meow

07:03.120 --> 07:06.930
and I'll redirect that into a cats.txt file.

07:06.930 --> 07:09.720
You could also just make that file yourself.

07:09.720 --> 07:11.190
Oh, I don't have the directory open here.

07:11.190 --> 07:14.520
I have just.gits. But anyway, I have a file.

07:14.520 --> 07:17.670
If we look at it, it says Meow Meow.

07:17.670 --> 07:19.950
All right, so now if I do a Git status

07:19.950 --> 07:24.693
I'll add that cats.txt and then I'll commit.

07:27.090 --> 07:29.133
Let's just go with add cats file.

07:30.360 --> 07:33.810
Okay, so now we have two commits, right?

07:33.810 --> 07:36.390
Our first one, but this new second one,

07:36.390 --> 07:40.620
if I copy this hash, first of all, 5119.

07:40.620 --> 07:42.870
We'll see that over here somewhere.

07:42.870 --> 07:44.793
I may need to refresh this.

07:45.720 --> 07:47.160
Where are you? Here it is.

07:47.160 --> 07:48.303
5119.

07:49.380 --> 07:52.650
All right, so this is that commit object stored here.

07:52.650 --> 07:57.650
Okay, so if we use cat file, Git cat dash file,

07:58.080 --> 08:01.320
we do dash T, we see this is indeed a commit.

08:01.320 --> 08:03.513
But if I do the same thing with dash p.

08:04.470 --> 08:07.170
Now we see it has a parent commit

08:07.170 --> 08:10.140
and this parent commit is the only other commit we have.

08:10.140 --> 08:12.840
Hopefully you remember it was 79DD.

08:12.840 --> 08:13.673
That is correct.

08:13.673 --> 08:16.020
It's referencing the commit that we made first,

08:16.020 --> 08:17.160
there's only two.

08:17.160 --> 08:20.970
So this was the first one and it has a reference to a tree.

08:20.970 --> 08:22.260
And this is a different tree.

08:22.260 --> 08:27.260
If I inspect this tree, Git cat dash file dash p

08:27.600 --> 08:29.070
and then I don't need the whole thing,

08:29.070 --> 08:31.473
but just a part of this hash for the tree.

08:32.520 --> 08:34.950
Now we see it has two blobs.

08:34.950 --> 08:35.940
Okay?

08:35.940 --> 08:37.830
So the first tree from the first commit,

08:37.830 --> 08:39.928
that snapshot, had one blob for one dogs file.

08:39.928 --> 08:42.240
That's all we had.

08:42.240 --> 08:45.210
This one, this tree has two blobs.

08:45.210 --> 08:47.280
But what's important to note,

08:47.280 --> 08:49.410
we didn't change this dogs file.

08:49.410 --> 08:54.090
So Git doesn't give us a new blob, it keeps the same object,

08:54.090 --> 08:57.480
it has the same hash, meaning it didn't change.

08:57.480 --> 09:00.390
I can prove this. So we have FD91.

09:00.390 --> 09:02.790
If I go look at this other commit,

09:02.790 --> 09:05.010
so the parent commit this is gonna be, ugh,

09:05.010 --> 09:07.740
I'm scared to do this 'cause it's just so many commit hashes

09:07.740 --> 09:10.440
and so many crazy looking shaw hashes.

09:10.440 --> 09:13.890
But if I look at this earlier commit, the first one,

09:13.890 --> 09:18.423
Git cat file dash p, and then we look at that tree,

09:19.890 --> 09:22.860
Git cat file dash p with a tree.

09:22.860 --> 09:24.000
What do we see?

09:24.000 --> 09:26.370
One blob, this was the first snapshot I made.

09:26.370 --> 09:30.270
We only had the dog's file, FD91.

09:30.270 --> 09:33.420
So here, my second commit, we looked at the tree,

09:33.420 --> 09:37.590
the snapshot, and there was one blob for cats, that's new,

09:37.590 --> 09:41.850
but the second blob is for dogs and it still is FD915

09:41.850 --> 09:43.920
because it never changed.

09:43.920 --> 09:46.410
But now if I change that file,

09:46.410 --> 09:48.780
right, I do something different in the dog's file,

09:48.780 --> 09:51.090
like I change it to be Sirius Black,

09:51.090 --> 09:52.740
character from Harry Potter.

09:52.740 --> 09:55.350
So that's the new contents of the dogs file.

09:55.350 --> 09:58.770
Well now if I add that change, Git add dogs,

09:58.770 --> 10:02.703
Git commit dash M, update dog's file.

10:04.680 --> 10:07.200
If I commit, well, I just did commit

10:07.200 --> 10:09.240
and I look at that new commit,

10:09.240 --> 10:12.900
this one here, 4EC and so on,

10:12.900 --> 10:17.463
if I take a look with Git cat dash file dash p,

10:18.570 --> 10:20.790
all right, it's referencing the parent commit.

10:20.790 --> 10:23.670
That was the second commit, 5119,

10:23.670 --> 10:25.620
just remember those numbers.

10:25.620 --> 10:29.700
And this tree now, 0E37, blah blah.

10:29.700 --> 10:34.350
If we inspect that cat file dash P, it's still two blobs.

10:34.350 --> 10:36.450
One for dogs and one for cats.

10:36.450 --> 10:39.810
But notice here, 04998,

10:39.810 --> 10:43.620
that is a new hash for the dog's blob,

10:43.620 --> 10:46.953
versus, if we go back up to the earlier one,

10:48.090 --> 10:51.933
it used to be, so many, here we are, FD915.

10:53.220 --> 10:55.800
So Git's not going to come up with new hashes

10:55.800 --> 10:58.560
and change these objects if it doesn't need to,

10:58.560 --> 11:00.360
if nothing changed in those files,

11:00.360 --> 11:01.830
the contents are the same.

11:01.830 --> 11:04.350
A blob stores the contents of a file.

11:04.350 --> 11:08.340
So right now Git is storing this version of the file

11:08.340 --> 11:12.000
in one blob and then it's storing this version of the file

11:12.000 --> 11:13.620
in another blob.

11:13.620 --> 11:17.700
And then the trees will reference one of those versions

11:17.700 --> 11:20.370
depending on what was in that snapshot

11:20.370 --> 11:22.380
when I made the commit.

11:22.380 --> 11:26.790
So I'm hoping maybe some of this is starting to make sense.

11:26.790 --> 11:29.520
If not, you know, the good news is you could still use Git

11:29.520 --> 11:31.800
and not have to worry about this.

11:31.800 --> 11:34.170
But this is about as far as I want to go

11:34.170 --> 11:36.180
in exploring all of this.

11:36.180 --> 11:38.280
There are other things we can do.

11:38.280 --> 11:41.430
We can actually make commits and generate trees

11:41.430 --> 11:44.460
in the same way that we created blobs from scratch.

11:44.460 --> 11:46.890
But I think this is a good place to stop.

11:46.890 --> 11:47.760
Okay?

11:47.760 --> 11:50.490
So every commit includes a reference to a parent,

11:50.490 --> 11:53.040
unless it's the first commit, there is no parent.

11:53.040 --> 11:54.780
And then a tree,

11:54.780 --> 11:57.660
so that's another type of Git object we've seen.

11:57.660 --> 12:01.710
The tree will contain a bunch of references potentially,

12:01.710 --> 12:06.000
to other trees, if it's a subdirectory or to blobs.

12:06.000 --> 12:08.940
And a blob contains file contents.

12:08.940 --> 12:11.130
So a tree is the place where we store

12:11.130 --> 12:14.880
or Git stores the file name corresponding to a blob.

12:14.880 --> 12:16.080
And then everything's hashed.

12:16.080 --> 12:19.800
Blobs get hashed, trees get hashed, commits get hashed.

12:19.800 --> 12:23.280
And Git uses those hashes to easily determine

12:23.280 --> 12:24.540
if something has changed.

12:24.540 --> 12:27.000
Because you change a single character in a file,

12:27.000 --> 12:28.740
that blobs hashes different.

12:28.740 --> 12:30.330
You change something in a directory,

12:30.330 --> 12:32.550
that tree's hash is different.

12:32.550 --> 12:35.760
But also it uses those hashes as keys

12:35.760 --> 12:37.980
so that it can easily retrieve things, right?

12:37.980 --> 12:41.190
Keys are supposed to be unique in a key value data store.

12:41.190 --> 12:44.010
So if I say "Git, give me the information

12:44.010 --> 12:46.140
from this commit, FA4."

12:46.140 --> 12:47.790
It can easily go and find that.

12:47.790 --> 12:50.370
Or give me the information for this blob.

12:50.370 --> 12:54.240
We usually don't do that on a blob basis, but Git does that.

12:54.240 --> 12:57.030
Git will restore contents in a directory

12:57.030 --> 12:59.400
based upon the tree in a commit.

12:59.400 --> 13:00.930
So when I switch branches,

13:00.930 --> 13:03.150
it's going to look at the tip of a branch,

13:03.150 --> 13:04.500
what's that commit?

13:04.500 --> 13:07.410
Okay, what's the tree in that commit?

13:07.410 --> 13:10.530
Let's take that and we're going to restore

13:10.530 --> 13:11.820
the files and the working directory

13:11.820 --> 13:14.250
based upon the state in that tree.

13:14.250 --> 13:16.470
So that's kind of the underlying workflow

13:16.470 --> 13:17.520
that Git goes through.

13:17.520 --> 13:19.590
Now we're skipping a lot of stuff,

13:19.590 --> 13:20.730
but that's the basic idea,

13:20.730 --> 13:24.360
with Git objects, with hashing, commits, trees, blobs.

13:24.360 --> 13:27.360
There's also this fourth type, the annotated tag.

13:27.360 --> 13:30.570
We have talked about tags and annotated tags.

13:30.570 --> 13:31.800
Not a big deal.

13:31.800 --> 13:34.230
As this image loads here. (chuckles)

13:34.230 --> 13:35.760
They're not as important right now,

13:35.760 --> 13:37.290
to understanding how all this works

13:37.290 --> 13:38.820
but it's another type of Git object.

13:38.820 --> 13:41.730
They're hashed, they're stored in the objects directory.

13:41.730 --> 13:45.810
They include metadata, who made the tag, the dates,

13:45.810 --> 13:49.590
a tag message, just like a commit, except that a tag

13:49.590 --> 13:52.890
is just a pointer to a particular commit.

13:52.890 --> 13:54.930
It is not an actual commit itself.

13:54.930 --> 13:57.330
So it kind of stores a reference to a commit.

13:57.330 --> 13:58.587
And a commit stores a reference to a tree

13:58.587 --> 14:01.230
and a tree stores a references to blobs.

14:01.230 --> 14:03.750
Alright, I applaud you if you made it through all of this.

14:03.750 --> 14:05.643
I hope some of it made sense.
